00:38:57,880 --> 00:39:00,780
So we might scale up volunteers again.

432
00:39:01,600 --> 00:39:10,580
But generally, up to about now, our programs have been run through working with volunteers, the same as they were at GRM.

433
00:39:11,220 --> 00:39:15,400
Right on. Well, maybe you can recruit Jason to get back over there after his next work hitch.

434
00:39:16,140 --> 00:39:23,460
Well, yeah, I'll speak to some of the volunteer greatness that happened when I volunteered in lots of places around the world.

435
00:39:23,620 --> 00:39:31,120
And a lot of the times you have to pay for your own flight there, pay for your own place to stay, meals and all that.

436
00:39:31,120 --> 00:39:34,800
And I mean, I felt like a king working for GRM.

437
00:39:34,800 --> 00:39:45,800
They actually took care of the flights and I believe they use that company Air Link, which is probably one of the unsung heroes of this whole medical response to Ukraine.

438
00:39:46,600 --> 00:39:50,300
They do advertise and to their own horn on occasion.

439
00:39:50,480 --> 00:39:56,360
But really, they're they're finding ways to get people over there so the companies don't have to pay.

440
00:39:56,360 --> 00:40:02,320
and uh you know they gave us a little bit of money for food every day which is more than enough for

441
00:40:02,320 --> 00:40:07,260
what we needed and uh gave us a place to sleep every night so that was very nice you know we

442
00:40:07,260 --> 00:40:11,280
didn't have to sleep in the ambulance so of all the places i volunteered it was one of the nicest

443
00:40:11,280 --> 00:40:17,500
you know and uh other than the occasional air raid alarm and you guys had a pretty cool apartment

444
00:40:17,500 --> 00:40:24,700
there in lavives you know what i actually still have i rented that same apartment again on behalf

445
00:40:24,700 --> 00:40:26,540
I liked it.

446
00:40:26,820 --> 00:40:29,040
It was great. It was really great.

447
00:40:30,660 --> 00:40:33,140
Yeah, that was definitely a super cool spot.

448
00:40:35,640 --> 00:40:38,980
Okay, so the most important question I have for you, Sarah,

449
00:40:39,120 --> 00:40:41,920
is when are you going to go to medic school?

450
00:40:42,880 --> 00:40:44,240
I was waiting for that one.

451
00:40:46,540 --> 00:40:47,620
What's the game plan?

452
00:40:48,400 --> 00:40:53,080
It's not something that I'm specifically looking to do

453
00:40:53,080 --> 00:40:54,280
at this point in time.

454
00:40:54,700 --> 00:40:58,840
Wait, sorry, I can't hear you. I think you said that you're not looking to do it.

455
00:41:00,900 --> 00:41:09,640
Right now, I think my long-term goals are sort of sticking around with humanitarianism, doing project management, things like that.

456
00:41:09,960 --> 00:41:16,200
However, you probably could argue that I've got a little bit of the medical bug now.

457
00:41:16,440 --> 00:41:20,660
So I guess come back and ask me in two years and we'll see.

458
00:41:21,520 --> 00:41:22,460
All right, perfect.

459
00:41:24,700 --> 00:41:27,860
I think those are all the questions that I had specifically for you guys.

460
00:41:27,960 --> 00:41:36,460
I don't know if either of you had anything else you wanted to mention about the experience over there in Ukraine or just like working abroad in this capacity in general.

461
00:41:37,280 --> 00:41:39,220
But I guess I guess I'll just open up to you guys.

462
00:41:43,580 --> 00:41:47,400
I'll go first. I think it was it was a positive experience.

463
00:41:47,400 --> 00:41:55,800
maybe too much so because I don't even talk about this experience with anyone.

464
00:41:59,240 --> 00:42:01,440
Yeah, it just really touched me.

465
00:42:02,620 --> 00:42:07,420
I mean, yeah, so they had a great leadership over there, believe it or not.

466
00:42:07,420 --> 00:42:14,080
It was an organization, I think GRM, and, you know, you guys saw a lot more behind the scenes,

467
00:42:14,080 --> 00:42:20,600
but for the grunt on the ground, which is what I was, we had really good leadership there.

468
00:42:20,740 --> 00:42:21,800
So good job, Sarah.

469
00:42:22,180 --> 00:42:23,480
You did a great job.

470
00:42:23,900 --> 00:42:24,680
Thanks, Jason.

471
00:42:24,840 --> 00:42:29,940
I think you're really, obviously, from the people that wanted to come back and keep working,

472
00:42:30,080 --> 00:42:31,020
you did a great job.

473
00:42:33,420 --> 00:42:38,080
But yeah, if you can make it over there and help out in some way, it'll change your life

474
00:42:38,080 --> 00:42:38,700
for sure.

475
00:42:39,260 --> 00:42:42,420
To see that, the suffering that was going on.

476
00:42:44,080 --> 00:42:46,260
Yeah, it touched me.

477
00:42:47,640 --> 00:42:49,880
Yeah, it's definitely a cool experience.

478
00:42:50,640 --> 00:42:58,040
And I think it's awesome that we managed to recruit another human into the EMS family via the whole thing.

479
00:42:58,480 --> 00:42:59,120
That's rad.

480
00:43:02,120 --> 00:43:04,840
Yeah, I guess I'll just mirror what Jason was saying.

481
00:43:04,960 --> 00:43:09,220
It's been just a really, really rewarding experience.

482
00:43:09,220 --> 00:43:14,000
and it's being out here it really feels like you can actually

483
00:43:14,000 --> 00:43:19,520
see this objective tangible benefit to the work in which you're doing

484
00:43:19,520 --> 00:43:23,200
and it's just really really awesome

485
00:43:23,200 --> 00:43:29,480
yeah for some really really appreciative people

486
00:43:29,480 --> 00:43:33,940
um you know here in the u.s we're used to working for people that expect

487
00:43:33,940 --> 00:43:37,980
good good health care they expect when they call 9-1-1 that they're going to

488
00:43:37,980 --> 00:43:41,980
have somebody show up that knows what they're doing and can take them to a higher level of care

489
00:43:41,980 --> 00:43:47,100
and those people are going to know what they're doing. They don't always have those expectations

490
00:43:47,100 --> 00:43:52,540
there. They'll take what they can get because they know their country's in a war. So over there,

491
00:43:52,600 --> 00:43:58,980
any type of little help, it just blew their minds, you know, that they would, oh, you actually have

492
00:43:58,980 --> 00:44:03,500
medicines for us to help us out with the pain on this long transport? It blew their mind because

493
00:44:03,500 --> 00:44:07,960
they weren't expecting that. They were expecting to have an X-Fix bouncing down the road and

494
00:44:07,960 --> 00:44:09,060
in a lot of pain, you know?

495
00:44:11,720 --> 00:44:12,660
Yeah, there's this,

496
00:44:12,900 --> 00:44:15,220
I think there's this cultural tolerance

497
00:44:15,220 --> 00:44:16,900
of pain.

498
00:44:17,560 --> 00:44:19,660
I had my wisdom teeth removed out here

499
00:44:19,660 --> 00:44:21,060
and they were just like,

500
00:44:21,140 --> 00:44:21,980
take ibuprofen.

501
00:44:25,080 --> 00:44:26,260
Where did you have it done?

502
00:44:26,320 --> 00:44:27,540
I remember you talking about that.

503
00:44:28,440 --> 00:44:30,220
I got it done at some place in Lviv.

504
00:44:31,040 --> 00:44:31,440
Awesome.

505
00:44:32,280 --> 00:44:34,800
And I think it cost about $300 in the end.

506
00:44:36,480 --> 00:44:37,100
That's sweet.

507
00:44:37,200 --> 00:44:37,920
And you didn't die?

508
00:44:37,960 --> 00:44:40,220
I didn't die.

509
00:44:40,660 --> 00:44:40,980
Perfect.

510
00:44:41,640 --> 00:44:42,440
Surprise, surprise.

511
